  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cooler through mid week.\n\nA notable change in the pattern takes place this evening as a \nsecondary surge arrives. This ushers in a period of brisk winds \nalong with falling temperatures and dew points. The result is a \nreturn of seasonably cold overnight conditions. Tonight's lows \nwill mainly be in the 30s east of the Blue Ridge, with mid/upper\n20s off to the west.\n\nThe temperature forecast for Tuesday and Wednesday are almost \nmirror images of one another. Daily highs range from the mid 40s\nto mid 50s, locally a bit cooler in the mountains. Between the \ntwo days, Tuesday will provide ample sunshine as a broad surface\nhigh tracks from the Midwest. By Tuesday night, a stout 1042-mb\nridge sits across New England which will make for a cold night \nover the local area. Forecast lows fall into the mid teens to \nmid 20s. For those along and east of I-95, upper 20s to near \nfreezing temperatures are more likely. Heading into Wednesday, \nskies will cloud up through the day ahead of the potent \nThanksgiving Day system. Any impacts largely hold off until \nWednesday night as northeasterly winds pick up in earnest. \nAlthough timing varies between models, gusty showers are \nforecast to lift northward across the area overnight. For more \ninformation on this storm, check out Key Message 2 (below).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A coastal low pressure system will bring the \npotential for heavy rain, damaging winds, and coastal flooding,\nas well as a little snow and ice in the mountains late \nWednesday through Friday.\n\nA coastal low pressure system near the Southeast U.S. Coast \nWednesday night will become more developed and intensify as it \nmoves northward toward the Mid-Atlantic for Thanksgiving Day \ninto early Friday. The impacts of the coastal low pressure \nsystem will depend on the exact track of the coastal low, the \nconnection to the upper-level low in support, and timing moving \nup the coast. The general trends in the last few model cycles \nhave been slower, stronger, and a bit further west, but still \nwith moderate spread.\n\nLight to moderate rain should move into southern parts of our region \novernight Wednesday into early Thanksgiving as the coastal low is \nover the eastern Carolinas. Increasing northeast to easterly winds\nwill also occur overnight Wednesday and become quite gusty heading\ninto Thanksgiving Day. As the winds remain strong to perhaps \ndamaging, rainfall could become a prolonged period of moderate \nto heavy rainfall on Thanksgiving. The origin of part of this \nsystem in the tropical East Pacific into the Caribbean along \nwith strong to extreme moisture transport in intense onshore \nlow-level winds approaching 80 knots may lead to very efficient\nrainfall rates despite limited instability (MUCAPE less than \n250 J/kg). The heaviest rain would most likely occur as the \ncoastal low moves out of eastern North Carolina and into \nsoutheastern Virginia. It would be at this point in time that \neverything is occurring all at once...strong low pressure \nnearby, moderate to heavy rainfall leading to 1 to 3 inches \ntotal, sustained winds of 15 to 30 mph gusting 35 to 50 mph or \nso, minor to moderate coastal flooding along the western shores \nof the Chesapeake Bay and the tidal Potomac River, and slightly \nabove average temperatures.\n\nMuch of the rainfall should taper or come to an end from southwest \nto northeast, winds diminishing and becoming more southwesterly as \nthe low pressure exits, and temperatures start to fall gradually. \nCoastal flooding may still be a factor as it may take a little more \ntime to recede once the low exits.\n\nA little rain and snow mix, or light snow may unfold overnight \nThursday into Friday over the Alleghenies as the secondary low \npressure system moves in place of the coastal low and starts to \npush out to the east. It's a little early to see how much snow \naccumulation could occur, but with the unfavorable (south-of-\nwest) wind direction, upper low support, and colder air, there \nshould be some accumulation on the mountaintops.",
